<title>RUSSIA: AGROKHLEB-Russian farm grain stocks very tight.</title>
<headline>AGROKHLEB-Russian farm grain stocks very tight.</headline>
<text>
<p>The following is a direct contribution from the Agrokhleb Report, published by SovEcon Ltd, an independent research organisation specialising in agricultural production and trade in the former Soviet Union.</p>
<p>RUSSIAN FARM GRAIN SUPPLIES REMAIN VERY TIGHT</p>
<p>MOSCOW, Oct 29 - By October 1, farm grain stocks had increased to 28.8 million tonnes but remained 3.6 million tonnes below year-ago levels.</p>
<p>Normally, farm grain stocks in Russia reach their peak by early October.</p>
<p>Though stocks may increase further in October due to delays in the harvest in western and eastern Siberia, they are expected to rise to only 30 million tonnes -- so they will be 1-2 million tonnes down on last year, when the grain harvest was the lowest since 1963.</p>
<p>In the Northern Caucasus and Central Chernozem (Black Earth) regions, which are usually the major grain surplus regions, total farm grain stocks are lower than last year, particularly in the Northern Caucasus (Table 1).</p>
<p>In addition, declines in farm grain supplies (year-on-year) are expected in western Siberia, eastern Siberia and the Central region.</p>
<p>Farm grain supplies are expected to increase only in Povolzhsk, the Urals and Volgo-Vyatsk regions.</p>
<p>As a result, despite some increase in grain output in 1996, the amount of marketable grains is likely to remain at last year's drought-reduced level, with domestic prices set to grow.</p>
